{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791118719,"nonce":"u57gyCOmKtql2mu6jYtCTDlJriY=","title":"Text nodes report no baseline to layout, so align-items: baseline aligns bottoms","message":"Text leaves are measured by blinc_layout's measure function, which returns a size and no first baseline. Taffy then takes a text node's bottom edge as its baseline, so `align-items: baseline` lines up the bottoms of line boxes rather than their baselines.\n\nFound with list markers: li { align-items: baseline } put the marker's bottom on the bottom of the content's first line box. In prose, where the content has a 1.75 line height and the marker 1.2, the bullet sat about 2px below the middle of the lowercase letters. Lists now top-align marker and content on a shared line height instead.\n\nRows that mix text sizes, such as a large number beside a small unit, or a label beside an input, have the same problem.\n\nFix: report the first baseline from the text measure, half-leading plus ascent for the first line, and pass it to Taffy as the leaf's first_baselines. That is Blinc's measure function (blinc_layout, text_measure / tree.rs) or a measure ashui supplies in its binding.","files":null}]}